 ## check.sh
 
-### §check-fails-open — the check never blocks a commit because of its own failure
+### §check-fails-closed — any failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit
+- Since: 2026-10-23
+- Supersedes: §check-fails-open (2026-09-11), overridden by the repository maintainer.
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. Any failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit status. Success still ends
+  in `exit 0`; large staged files are only warned about.
+- Forbidden: dropping `-e`, and swallowing internal errors to force `exit 0`.
+- Reason: the maintainer prefers a check that fails loudly over one that silently continues after its own errors.
+- Known risk (from the superseded record): with `-e`, a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborted the check
+  and blocked every commit in the repository for a day (2026-09-10). The `[ -f "$f" ] || continue` guard covers the
+  deleted-file case; other internal failures will now block the commit.
+- Implementation note: the large-file test uses `if`, not `[ ... ] && echo`, because a false `&&` list as the last
+  command of the loop would make the pipeline fail under `-e`/`pipefail` on every normal run.
+
+### §check-fails-open — SUPERSEDED by §check-fails-closed
 - Since: 2026-09-11
-- Decision: `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ends in `exit 0`; the script only warns.
-- Forbidden: `set -e` or `set -o errexit`, and any exit status other than 0.
-- Reason: with `set -e`, a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborted the check and blocked every commit in
-  the repository for a day (2026-09-10). The check is advisory by design.
+- Decision (historical): `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`; every internal error ended in `exit 0`.
